    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay, so you mentioned the 14th amendment and the \"public vs private\" thing. but it feels like most historians and legal experts actually find those arguments pretty flawed or even logically unsound when you look at how systemic the harm was. i mean, if the arguments are basically just ways to avoid responsibility, shouldn't an objective overview point out why they're considered invalid in modern scholarship, rather than just listing them as \"substantial claims\"?",
